  "id": "u10-l02",
  "unit": "u10-culture",
  "title": "Ramadan & iftar",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "During **রমজান** (Ramadan), the ninth month of the Islamic calendar, most Bangladeshi Muslims fast from dawn to sunset, with no food or drink. Bengali says **রোজা রাখি**, literally 'I keep the fast'. It's the same verb you met in the bazaar (**কম রাখেন!**): **আমি রোজা রাখি** 'I fast', **আপনি কি রোজা রাখেন?** 'Do you fast?'\n\nThe day is framed by two meals: **সেহরি** before dawn and **ইফতার** at sunset. Many people break the fast with a **খেজুর** (date). A classic Dhaka iftar also has *chhola* (spiced chickpeas), *peyaju* (lentil-and-onion fritters), *beguni* (aubergine fritters) and *jilapi*.\n\nEndings to notice: **রমজানে** 'in Ramadan', **ইফতারে** 'at iftar'. **ইফতার** also pairs with **করি** 'do': **আমি এখনো ইফতার করিনি** 'I haven't had iftar yet'.\n\nBeing invited to iftar is a warm gesture of friendship, and non-Muslim friends are welcome too. Asking **আপনি কি রোজা রাখেন?** is polite, but it is a personal question, so don't press. Ramadan ends with *Eid-ul-Fitr* (Lesson 1).",
